  "subtitle": "A synthesizer in a briefcase whose programs are pins and whose keyboard is a touchplate",
  "why_scout_cares": "The EMS Synthi AKS (March 1972, £420 launch; £1,452 in the 1979 catalogue, production through c.1984) is the museum's strangest music machine and one of its strangest interfaces: a complete synthesizer studio in a padded briefcase whose two signature input methods are both physically bizarre. The synth face is a pin-matrix patch panel — a grid of ~16×16 sockets into which you push resistive pins to route signals, so the 'program' is literally the physical arrangement of pins on the board, a plugboard/tangible-routing interface descended from telephone exchanges. In the lid sits a 30-note, 2.5-octave flat capacitive touch keyboard with no moving keys — you trigger notes by touching a printed board (finger capacitance of 10–100 pF), and a 256-step digital sequencer is programmed by touching the Record pad and playing in real time. Alan Parsons confirmed the sequence on Pink Floyd's 'On the Run' was 'laboriously programmed using the AKS's membrane keyboard' and played back at high speed. The museum has knobs and drum pads and light pens, but nothing where inserting pins routes your sounds and a printed touchplate is your keyboard.",
